PlatformIO tests for the Gravity FlexSeq project.

This directory holds THREE categories of test that must never be mixed. They live
in three distinct PlatformIO environments, and that is deliberately structural:
it is what lets the acceptance suite stay a reliable signal.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. FlexSeq acceptance / regression  ->  env `native`
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    test_cv_gate, test_main_screen, test_paged_screen, test_pattern,
    test_pattern_bank, test_pattern_screen, test_persistence,
    test_ratchet_matrix, test_sequencer_engine, test_subdiv, test_transport,
    test_subdiv_phase, test_trigger_sequencer, test_ui_controller

Pure C++ domain, no Arduino and no libGravity. MUST be green: a phase is not
finished while one of these fails. 317 assertions as of 2026-08-23, counted
from the `RUN_TEST` calls and confirmed by the runner's own total.

⚠️ **`onsetCount()` is not what the outputs see.** The engine counts step and
sub-step crossings, so it reports the onsets of an **inactive** step too, ratchets
included. `TriggerSequencer::triggerCount()` is what filters on the step being
active, and it is what `main.cpp` drives the pins with. An assertion about "an
inactive step emits nothing" therefore belongs on the sequencer, never on the
engine. Found by measurement on 2026-08-23; the header comment on `onsetCount()`
still says "used to drive the outputs" and is stale.

`test_ratchet_matrix` characterizes the ratchet-by-SUBDIV matrix: the step
duration for all 25 rates, the trigger count for the 125 rate-by-code pairs, the
tick gaps of the trigger train, and the four rules the owner fixed on 2026-08-23
-- an inactive step emits nothing, an inactive triplet is a triplet rest, a
ratchet survives its step being switched off, and clearing a pattern wipes steps
and ratchets together. It records the CURRENT behaviour, including the 13 pairs
that fold to one trigger: lot 21 replaces that rule, and these assertions make
the change visible instead of silent.

Each of the four added since 2026-08-22 was verified by MUTATION, not merely by
being green: test_ui_controller 22/22 mutations detected, test_main_screen 20/20,
test_persistence 17/17 then 16/16 for the version 2 format, and the footer
additions to test_paged_screen and test_pattern_screen 7/8 -- the undetected
mutation there being an equivalent mutant, which ADR 0001 records rather than
counts as a pass. Their TypeScript mirrors were swept the same way (15/15,
15/15, 22/22).


⚠️ **`npm run typecheck` catches what the TypeScript suite cannot.** vitest does
not typecheck: when lot 19 removed `UiEvent.RotateHeld`, the tests still using it
kept **passing**, because the member evaluated to `undefined`, `handle(undefined,
1)` did nothing, and the next assertion measured that absence of effect. `tsc
--noEmit` named it at once. The same mistake is a compilation error on the C++
side. The typecheck is **not** part of `run-all-tests.sh`: run it after any change
to a shared type.

⚠️ **A new test module must be added to `test_filter`, or it stays silent.**
`env:native` carries an allow-list, and `test_subdiv` has no wildcard, so
`test_subdiv_phase` ran when it was named and was **not collected by the full
suite**. This is the same trap that commit `f0d6134` set for four months. The
list cannot become `test_*`: the libGravity characterization tests live in the
same directory and belong to another environment.

⚠️ **`Rig::gotoTab()` is bounded, and it must stay bounded.** It used to be a
`while` loop over `EVENT_ROTATE`. A mutant that made that rotation inert turned
the loop into an infinite one, and the mutation probe then reported the mutation
detected by its deadline instead of by the assertion that covers it. The method
rule this produced lives in `docs/open-risks.md`, under the method rules.

    ./tools/run-cpp-tests.sh [test_name]

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Input-adapter acceptance  ->  env `native_adapter`
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    test_input_adapter

`./tools/run-adapter-tests.sh`. OUR glue code, compiled against the Arduino stubs
in test/mocks/ and libGravity's headers. MUST be green.

What only this env can test: the policy that decides a hold used for a rotation
is NOT a long press. It depends on time and on pin state, so the domain cannot
see it -- the domain only receives events that were already decided. The defect
it caught was real and reached the module: holding SHIFT to turn the encoder fired
EVENT_SHIFT_LONG_PRESS on release, which CLEARED THE PATTERN.

The test compiles src/hal/InputAdapter.cpp into its own translation unit, with an
#include, and it has to. test/mocks/Arduino.h declares its pin and time state as
`static` at header scope, so EVERY TRANSLATION UNIT GETS ITS OWN COPY: a test
writing setDigitalPin in its copy would drive nothing at all. test_gravity
includes libGravity.cpp for the same reason.

gravity.Init() is what gives the buttons their pin. Without it, shift_button
reads pin 0 instead of pin 12 and every SHIFT gesture is silent. The rig calls
Init on both buttons.

One finding this bench produced, and it is a bench artefact: the first main-loop
pass delivered a spurious encoder press, because Button samples old_read_ at
global construction, when the mock has every pin at zero. Checked on the module
on 2026-08-23 -- IT DOES NOT HAPPEN THERE: one detent moves the selected tab, so
the UI is still on the tab bar.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. libGravity characterization  ->  env `native_libgravity`
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    test_analog_input, test_button, test_clock, test_digital_output,
    test_encoder, test_gravity

libGravity headers (pinned at commit 9be88be1f4) compiled against the Arduino
stubs in `test/mocks/`. These tests describe the REAL behaviour of the pinned
dependency, defects included: 7 assertions out of 68 fail by construction.
Never weaken them to obtain green — that would hide a defect of the dependency.

Since 2026-08-20, `test_gravity` additionally CHARACTERIZES `Gravity::Process()`:
it reads exactly two analog inputs — CV1 and CV2, once each — and polls both
buttons plus the encoder switch. FlexSeq no longer calls that function, the
converter now belonging to an ISR (PRD 10.6). So the risk is not missing an
upstream change — the dependency is pinned by decision — but forgetting to
re-audit that function at the next pin bump. These assertions turn that
oversight into a failure. A twin test verifies that the pieces called instead
cover the same inputs with ZERO analog reads: that is the invariant which makes
the workaround correct. The per-pin read counters live in `test/mocks/Arduino.h`.

The criterion for this suite is therefore NOT "everything passes", it is
"conforms to the audit". A module that reproduces exactly the audited anomalies
is GREEN. Red is reserved for drift: a failure outside the reference, or an
audited anomaly that starts passing — which would mean the pinned dependency
changed under our feet.

The three ways of making those 7 assertions green are all closed off: patching
the dependency (forbidden, it is pinned), weakening the tests (forbidden, and
that is what hid the breakage for four months), or working around it on the
FlexSeq side — which is the adapter layer's job, but changes nothing about
libGravity's behaviour, so these tests would stay red, and rightly so.

    ./tools/run-libgravity-tests.sh

The script does more than run the suite: it compares the set of failing
assertions against the audited reference and reports drift in either direction
(an anomaly that disappears, one that appears). An expected red is not a signal;
a red that changes is one.

It produces its own report and MASKS the `pio test` transcript: a per-module
recap, then the list of reproduced anomalies with the libGravity defect each one
documents. `VERBOSE=1` restores the raw transcript, `VERBOSE=2` shows it with
`-vvv`.

Prerequisite: the AVR env must have been built once, the headers being read from
`.pio/libdeps/nanoatmega328/`. The script takes care of it when needed.

Why the pio transcript is masked — three of its outputs mislead on
`platform = native`:

  - it presents the Unity binary's exit code (= the number of failing
    assertions) as a signal: "SIGINT (Interrupt: 2)" for 2 failures, "SIGHUP
    (Hangup: 1)" for 1. Neither is a crash;
  - the resulting ERRORED status is therefore unusable, and it hides the
    distinction between "fails as expected" and "does not compile";
  - its total is wrong: it adds one pseudo-assertion per ERRORED module.

A practical annoyance on top: PlatformIO strips its colours as soon as its
output is captured, which makes PASSED and FAILED indistinguishable in a log.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Both, plus the TypeScript model
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh                # all three suites
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --cpp          # C++ acceptance only
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --ts           # TypeScript only
    ./tools/run-all-tests.sh --libgravity   # characterization only

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
History — why this README exists
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The six characterization tests were introduced by `0574600`, where the `native`
env carried `-I.pio/libdeps/nanoatmega328/libGravity/src -Itest/mocks`. Commit
`f0d6134` (PR #1) rewrote that env for the domain tests: it removed those two
`-I` flags AND added `test_filter = test_pattern*` in the same commit. The tests
therefore stopped compiling without ever being collected — so without emitting
any signal at all, for four months.

The lesson is structural: a `test_filter` that narrows collection can hide a
compilation failure. That is why the two categories now each have their own
environment, and why the characterization suite is run by default by
`run-all-tests.sh`.
